{
  "id": "integrator-op-amp",
  "title": "The Integrator Op-Amp: Square Wave In, Triangle Wave Out",
  "eyebrow": "operational amplifiers",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 58,
  "topic": "Op-amp integrator — replace the inverting amp's feedback resistor with a capacitor, and the virtual short turns the circuit into a time integrator: V_out(t) = -(1/RC)∫V_in dt. A square-wave input produces a linear triangle-wave output whose slope is set by 1/RC.",
  "subject_id": "ade",
  "chapter_number": 9,
  "prerequisites": [
    "inverting-op-amp"
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "manimoIntro",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 7.26,
      "narration": "What if the feedback path of an op-amp were a capacitor instead of a resistor? You'd build a time integrator.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "manimo-enter",
            "content": "Manimo bobs in centre-screen",
            "delay": 0
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Swap the feedback resistor for a capacitor — what do you get?",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "right of Manimo"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "configuration",
      "start": 7.26,
      "end": 19.77,
      "narration": "Same inverting topology: V plus grounded, input through R into V minus, feedback from V out back to V minus — but the feedback element is a capacitor C, not a resistor.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Op-amp triangle pointing right",
            "delay": 0.3,
