        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "derivation",
      "start": 19.77,
      "end": 32.52,
      "narration": "Virtual short forces V minus to ground. So the current through R is V in over R, and that same current charges the capacitor — meaning dV out over dt equals minus V in over R C.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "V₋ ≈ 0  (virtual ground)",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "step 1"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "i = V_in / R",
            "delay": 1.5,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "step 2"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "i = −C · dV_out/dt",
            "delay": 2.8,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "step 3"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "V_out(t) = −(1/RC) ∫ V_in dt",
            "delay": 4.5,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "headline payoff"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sweep",
      "start": 32.52,
      "end": 45.98,
      "narration": "Feed in a square wave. During each half cycle V in is constant, so V out ramps down at minus V in over R C — out comes a triangle wave whose slope is set entirely by R times C.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "graph-axes",
            "content": "Two stacked traces — V_in (top) and V_out (bottom)",
            "delay": 0.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-curve",
            "content": "V_in square wave — white step trace, top row",
            "delay": 0.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-curve",
            "content": "V_out triangle wave — amber, traces in synchronously below",
            "delay": 0.6,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "graph-marker",
            "content": "time cursor sweeping",
            "delay": 0.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "during each flat half-cycle, V_out ramps linearly",
            "delay": 9,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "below the chart"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "takeaway",
      "start": 45.98,
      "end": 58,
      "narration": "An op-amp integrator turns a square wave into a triangle wave, and more generally turns any input into its running time integral — with R times C setting the slope.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Output is the running integral of the input.",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "centre, serif italic"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "Slope = −V_in / RC",
            "delay": 1.6,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "below"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
